1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,040
They perform an awesome high wire act.

2
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:06,700
Only these wires rage with deadly currents.

3
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:13,380
Their mission is to keep the lights on every minute of every day.

4
00:00:14,460 --> 00:00:18,880
For over a century, they've climbed poles, scaled towers, and handled live wires.

5
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:24,360
They're an elite group of courageous workers known as electrical linemen.

6
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:27,980
It's high voltage on modern marbles.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
These are 500,000 volt power lines.

8
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:48,320
Wires that carry enough electricity to light an entire city.

9
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:56,480
Countless miles of lines like these move electricity from power plants to population centers.

10
00:00:56,481 --> 00:01:04,400
There, a complex network of substations, transformers and smaller wires distribute the essential

11
00:01:04,401 --> 00:01:08,720
fuel for modern life.

12
00:01:08,721 --> 00:01:14,720
Electricity travels at the speed of life, an ever-ready flow of invisible energy.

13
00:01:14,721 --> 00:01:21,660
But there is a fundamental principle of electricity that is constantly working to disrupt this

14
00:01:21,661 --> 00:01:23,160
system.

15
00:01:23,161 --> 00:01:26,160
the principle that makes electric current so dangerous.

16
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:32,760
Electricity always looks for the easiest path to the ground.

17
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:37,860
It normally travels smoothly along the power lines

18
00:01:37,861 --> 00:01:41,700
because insulators keep the wires from touching paths to the ground,

19
00:01:41,701 --> 00:01:43,260
such as poles or towers.

20
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:48,200
But sometimes a storm tears down a wire,

21
00:01:48,201 --> 00:01:50,520
or an earthquake topples a tower,

22
00:01:50,521 --> 00:01:56,660
or a person accidentally creates a path between the ground and a live wire by touching both at the same time.

23
00:01:57,360 --> 00:02:02,400
And then, in an instant, electricity chooses a deadly new route.

24
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:04,917
Electricity is very unforgiving.

25
00:02:04,918 --> 00:02:08,500
It doesn't care if it goes through a copper wire or a human being.

26
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:11,620
It just wants to get down to the ground. It wants to get back to the earth.

27
00:02:12,260 --> 00:02:14,199
It's not particular which path it takes.

28
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:17,640
It takes the easiest path, and sometimes that can be through your body.

29
00:02:17,641 --> 00:02:25,640
We all understand the impact of touching a household outlet carrying 120 volts, but the

30
00:02:25,641 --> 00:02:30,640
effect of contacting thousands of volts is beyond imagination.

31
00:02:30,641 --> 00:02:40,980
When you make contact, it usually runs down the veins in your body because your blood

32
00:02:40,981 --> 00:02:45,340
is salty and that's the way it runs down through there.

33
00:02:45,341 --> 00:02:47,980
It just cooks it. It'll cook your veins.

34
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:54,700
No one is more aware of this immense force

35
00:02:54,701 --> 00:02:57,880
than the workers responsible for maintaining the power system.

36
00:02:58,940 --> 00:03:00,000
Electrical linemen.

37
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:03,940
They know that just one tiny slip,

38
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:07,440
one momentary break in concentration,

39
00:03:08,060 --> 00:03:09,600
can be catastrophic.

40
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:13,860
When I first came to work at public service,

41
00:03:13,861 --> 00:03:15,560
I told them I wanted to be a lineman.

42
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:17,740
So they said, okay.

43
00:03:18,180 --> 00:03:19,540
I said, but just remember this.

44
00:03:20,180 --> 00:03:22,020
There's no eraser on a lineman's pencil.

45
00:03:22,620 --> 00:03:23,700
Don't ever make a mistake.

46
00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:26,340
You make a mistake, it's likely to be your last one.

47
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:33,280
In our line of work as linemen, I'd say 99.99% of the time,

48
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:35,720
you make one mistake, you don't go home to your family.

49
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:39,420
If you're not a fatality from the contact,

50
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:43,640
you're going to lose fingers and toes or whatever.

51
00:03:43,860 --> 00:03:46,300
despair and unforgiving.

52
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:50,060
You try not to fear it, you try to respect it.

53
00:03:50,220 --> 00:03:52,020
You don't get a second chance in this line of work.

54
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:55,040
I mean, this stuff, you know, it can take you out like that.

55
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:55,900
You know, it can kill you.

56
00:03:58,080 --> 00:03:59,160
Despite the risks,

57
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:02,440
linemen dedicate themselves to keeping the lights on.

58
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,460
And that means facing an array of challenges.

59
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:08,200
Whether it's climbing a wooden pole

60
00:04:08,201 --> 00:04:09,780
to hook up residential service,

61
00:04:09,781 --> 00:04:15,960
scaling a 10-story tower to change a broken insulator

62
00:04:15,961 --> 00:04:23,480
or braving a killer storm to restore service

63
00:04:23,481 --> 00:04:30,620
it may mean operating high-tech equipment to locate a cut in an underground line

64
00:04:32,960 --> 00:04:36,980
riding a giant power line in a windswept desert to make repairs

65
00:04:36,981 --> 00:04:44,940
or balancing on the skid of a helicopter to fix a live wire.

66
00:04:48,840 --> 00:04:51,420
Clearly, this is a perilous profession.

67
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:55,100
When Lyman first went to work over a century ago,

68
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:58,540
one out of every two were killed on the job.

69
00:05:00,980 --> 00:05:05,640
Today, meticulous safety procedures and exhaustive training programs

70
00:05:05,641 --> 00:05:11,960
dramatically reduced the risk, but linemen still face the ever-present specter of sudden death.

71
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:18,420
The most horrific kinds of assaults on the human body one can think of result from

72
00:05:18,421 --> 00:05:20,550
accidents, electrical accidents in the industry.

73
00:05:20,551 --> 00:05:22,680
So it's something that we need to be continually

74
00:05:22,681 --> 00:05:27,200
working on, continually aware of, and working with each generation of new workers so that

75
00:05:27,201 --> 00:05:29,640
The past experiences can never be forgotten.

76
00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:38,380
In 1879, Thomas Edison perfected the incandescent bulb.

77
00:05:39,560 --> 00:05:43,500
Now, for the first time, electric light was practical for the home.

78
00:05:45,020 --> 00:05:47,340
But Edison's contribution went much farther.

79
00:05:48,460 --> 00:05:53,600
At the same time, he developed generators, fuses, switches, sockets, and meters.

80
00:05:53,601 --> 00:05:57,360
all the equipment necessary to create an industry.

81
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:06,480
On September 4, 1882, he threw the switch at the Pearl Street Power Generating Station in New York City.

82
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:11,380
Thanks to some early linemen who had laid underground cable,

83
00:06:12,060 --> 00:06:15,660
59 lucky customers enjoyed the marvelous new creation.

84
00:06:17,020 --> 00:06:20,940
But Edison used a system called direct current, DC,

85
00:06:20,941 --> 00:06:23,940
which could only be transmitted several miles.

86
00:06:25,940 --> 00:06:31,440
Then in 1886, George Westinghouse, with the help of scientist Nikola Tesla,

87
00:06:32,100 --> 00:06:35,320
developed an alternating current, or AC system.

88
00:06:38,940 --> 00:06:42,460
With this technology, AC current is generated.

89
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:46,600
Then a transformer steps it up to extremely high voltage

90
00:06:46,601 --> 00:06:50,700
so it can be sent long distances on transmission lines.

91
00:06:50,940 --> 00:07:00,940
Transformers then step down the voltage so it can be sent on distribution lines to customers.

92
00:07:00,941 --> 00:07:06,340
Many flock to this exciting new occupation, traveling from town to town building the massive

93
00:07:06,341 --> 00:07:11,860
infrastructure necessary to deliver the powerful current.

94
00:07:11,861 --> 00:07:17,380
But at this time, very little was known about this mysterious force, and linemen quickly

95
00:07:17,381 --> 00:07:19,700
learned the deadly hazards of electricity.

96
00:07:21,980 --> 00:07:30,500
These early linemen were exposed to up to 2,400 volts and sometimes 7,600 volts, and it was

97
00:07:30,501 --> 00:07:35,240
a potentially lethal voltage, and it was a real sharp learning curve on exactly how to

98
00:07:35,241 --> 00:07:36,140
handle that voltage.

99
00:07:38,340 --> 00:07:43,320
Many linemen made contact and completed that fatal path to the ground.

100
00:07:43,321 --> 00:07:49,800
others learned that when a live wire touched a pole or another wire it

101
00:07:49,801 --> 00:07:58,620
created an intense ball of fire called a flash these linemen worked long hours

102
00:07:58,621 --> 00:08:06,120
for poor wages and safety procedures were non-existent fatalities ran twice as high

103
00:08:06,121 --> 00:08:07,780
as any other occupation.

104
00:08:10,320 --> 00:08:13,840
Then in 1891, a small group of linemen,

105
00:08:14,280 --> 00:08:16,020
outraged by their working conditions,

106
00:08:16,580 --> 00:08:19,360
formed the National Brotherhood of Electrical Workers.

107
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:25,280
Linemen realized they shared a unique fellowship

108
00:08:25,281 --> 00:08:27,620
dealing with their hazardous job.

109
00:08:30,120 --> 00:08:32,900
The union's first president, Henry Miller,

110
00:08:33,220 --> 00:08:34,440
was a respected lineman.

111
00:08:34,441 --> 00:08:39,440
He traveled the nation working on jobs and organizing linemen.

112
00:08:39,441 --> 00:08:44,440
Within a year, membership grew to 2,000.

113
00:08:44,441 --> 00:08:53,440
The union lobbied for new safety regulations and instituted a system of death benefits for families of workers who were killed.

114
00:08:53,441 --> 00:09:00,440
Ironically, this policy almost bankrupt the union because mortality rates were so high.

115
00:09:00,441 --> 00:09:03,580
But then, a tragic accident.

116
00:09:05,240 --> 00:09:09,260
In 1896, Henry Miller was working on a pole in Washington, D.C.

117
00:09:09,820 --> 00:09:15,580
when he made contact with a 2,200-volt line and fell to his death.

118
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:19,280
The union's founder was gone,

119
00:09:19,820 --> 00:09:24,040
but linemen banded together to train and protect their brothers.

120
00:09:26,760 --> 00:09:30,120
The word electric comes from the Greek word electron,

121
00:09:30,121 --> 00:09:31,360
which means amber.

122
00:09:31,900 --> 00:09:34,060
The ancient Greeks discovered that rubbing amber

123
00:09:34,061 --> 00:09:36,920
against another surface created static electricity.

124
00:09:37,620 --> 00:09:39,900
High Voltage will continue on Modern Marvels.

125
00:09:46,020 --> 00:09:48,080
By the early 1900s,

126
00:09:48,260 --> 00:09:50,340
the demand for electricity was exploding.

127
00:09:54,080 --> 00:09:56,520
Factories relied on power for their equipment,

128
00:09:56,521 --> 00:10:00,340
and exciting new electrical appliances were all the rage.

129
00:10:07,940 --> 00:10:12,340
The Union, now the International Brotherhood of Electrical Workers,

130
00:10:13,140 --> 00:10:15,340
started the first apprenticeship programs.

131
00:10:16,640 --> 00:10:19,940
However, formal training was still decades away.

132
00:10:20,880 --> 00:10:22,980
Back then it was on-the-job training.

133
00:10:22,981 --> 00:10:27,340
and you learn from experience and your mistakes.

134
00:10:28,580 --> 00:10:31,840
And that's about the only way you had any training back then.

135
00:10:35,120 --> 00:10:36,980
They never give us safety training.

136
00:10:37,100 --> 00:10:38,180
You learned it yourself.

137
00:10:39,580 --> 00:10:42,580
Whatever you wanted to do to be safe, well, that's what you did.

138
00:10:43,360 --> 00:10:48,080
The basic skill that all linemen had to master was climbing a wooden pole.

139
00:10:48,081 --> 00:10:53,960
The techniques for this treacherous job have changed very little over the years.

140
00:10:55,380 --> 00:11:02,120
Some of the tools we use to recline are the gas, the climbers, pole climbers.

141
00:11:02,560 --> 00:11:09,400
You strap these on your feet and the spikes penetrate the wood and give you a place to stand.

142
00:11:10,780 --> 00:11:13,260
If you do it right, you won't slip.

143
00:11:13,720 --> 00:11:15,120
And this takes a lot of practice too.

144
00:11:15,121 --> 00:11:18,360
You can't just strap on a set of these and climb up a pole.

145
00:11:18,361 --> 00:11:19,500
It takes a lot of practice.

146
00:11:19,501 --> 00:11:23,800
You've got your what's called a body belt.

147
00:11:23,801 --> 00:11:28,040
Once you get to the position where you're going to work, you put this around the pole,

148
00:11:28,041 --> 00:11:31,680
snap it off, and that holds you in position.

149
00:11:31,681 --> 00:11:36,800
If we don't know how old the pole is, you want to sound it out with a hammer.

150
00:11:36,801 --> 00:11:38,840
Make sure it's not rotten.

151
00:11:38,841 --> 00:11:43,200
If it sounds solid, then you can climb it.

152
00:11:43,201 --> 00:11:50,200
the gaff guards off and then just start climbing.

153
00:11:54,520 --> 00:11:57,799
Well my first climbing experience was kind of scary.

154
00:11:57,800 --> 00:11:59,880
We were only allowed to learn how

155
00:11:59,881 --> 00:12:04,971
to climb on the job during our lunch break. There really wasn't any instruction.

156
00:12:04,972 --> 00:12:05,672
You just

157
00:12:05,673 --> 00:12:10,520
put on, you barred the climbers that belonged to the linemen and you put them on and you

158
00:12:10,521 --> 00:12:13,280
climbed up and I climbed about eight or ten feet high and I didn't know how to

159
00:12:13,281 --> 00:12:15,760
get out.

160
00:12:15,761 --> 00:12:21,520
Linemen had to learn to let go with both hands and trust their safety belts.

161
00:12:21,521 --> 00:12:29,020
A risky proposition in the early days. Pole climbing remains today the first

162
00:12:29,021 --> 00:12:34,260
test to determine if a new lineman has what it takes. I don't think anyone just

163
00:12:34,261 --> 00:12:41,840
goes up there and leans back on a three-inch belt on a 50-foot pole with nothing below you and says,

164
00:12:41,940 --> 00:12:43,029
oh yeah, I'm okay.

165
00:12:43,030 --> 00:12:47,020
Most of us have been up there when that belt just slipped an inch.

166
00:12:47,760 --> 00:12:49,000
It felt like it went a mile.

167
00:12:54,080 --> 00:12:58,780
Throughout these early years, voltages increased to meet the demand for electricity,

168
00:12:59,140 --> 00:13:03,620
and turning off service to work on lines was an unpopular option.

169
00:13:04,260 --> 00:13:11,260
Linemen now had to develop new equipment for safe maintenance of live lines.

170
00:13:11,261 --> 00:13:16,260
This 1914 photo is the first known image of the wooden hot stick,

171
00:13:16,261 --> 00:13:21,260
a long pole with an array of interchangeable tools that can be fitted on the end.

172
00:13:21,261 --> 00:13:26,260
The hot stick is something that you take the place of your hands.

173
00:13:26,261 --> 00:13:30,260
You do all the work with the end of the stick and still your hands.

174
00:13:30,261 --> 00:13:32,200
that was on the higher voltages.

175
00:13:33,140 --> 00:13:38,400
And, boy, I don't know, you just get so used to it,

176
00:13:38,420 --> 00:13:42,780
you use a stick, you can do it better than your hands on a lot of jobs.

177
00:13:44,200 --> 00:13:47,760
The biggest enemy of one of these hot sticks is moisture

178
00:13:47,761 --> 00:13:50,600
because wood absorbs moisture.

179
00:13:50,800 --> 00:13:54,420
And just like anybody that steps into a bathtub knows,

180
00:13:54,500 --> 00:13:56,460
you don't want electricity around water.

181
00:13:56,461 --> 00:14:01,500
so with these hot sticks you want to make absolutely sure that you kept them

182
00:14:01,501 --> 00:14:07,560
very dry or this current could leak along the surface of these sticks to the

183
00:14:07,561 --> 00:14:13,540
point where it could be lethal by the 1950s wooden hot sticks were being

184
00:14:13,541 --> 00:14:18,420
replaced with fiberglass models they provided better insulation and they were

185
00:14:18,421 --> 00:14:24,000
lighter and easier to handle the hot stick remains today an important lineman's

186
00:14:24,001 --> 00:14:32,150
tool. Rubber gloves are another indispensable tool for handling live lines.

187
00:14:32,151 --> 00:14:33,780
They were first

188
00:14:33,781 --> 00:14:39,520
introduced around 1915, but the early gloves were vastly different from today's models.

189
00:14:40,900 --> 00:14:46,620
Rubber gloves that the linemen used to wear were what they called straight-hand rubber gloves.

190
00:14:47,080 --> 00:14:49,141
They were just perfectly straight.

191
00:14:49,142 --> 00:14:52,840
If you worked in them all day, your hand was so tired because

192
00:14:52,841 --> 00:14:56,902
they wouldn't bend. It was on a constant strain.

193
00:14:56,903 --> 00:15:00,880
Then in 1933, the White Rubber Company received

194
00:15:00,881 --> 00:15:05,323
a patent for the first glove designed specifically for linemen.

195
00:15:05,324 --> 00:15:07,580
White Rubber Company developed a

196
00:15:07,581 --> 00:15:14,040
curve hand glove, and it's just like your hand, and it made it so much more comfortable to wear

197
00:15:14,041 --> 00:15:16,398
that glove and work with it.

198
00:15:16,399 --> 00:15:21,620
A leather glove covers the rubber to guard against cutting the

199
00:15:21,621 --> 00:15:25,940
life-saving insulation.

200
00:15:25,941 --> 00:15:36,940
Today, rubber gloves are the primary method for handling live lines up to 34,500 volts.

201
00:15:38,140 --> 00:15:44,020
After World War II, the electric industry began a greater push for lineman safety.

202
00:15:44,021 --> 00:15:48,920
A study by the Edison Electric Institute revealed that the lineman's head touching the live

203
00:15:48,921 --> 00:15:53,640
wire caused 8% of all contact fatalities.

204
00:15:53,641 --> 00:15:56,680
Back then, you could wear any kind of hat you wanted to.

205
00:15:56,681 --> 00:16:01,400
Some of the linemen wore felt hats with high crowns.

206
00:16:01,401 --> 00:16:04,200
So if they bumped into anything, they could feel it.

207
00:16:04,201 --> 00:16:08,000
And it wouldn't shock them, because it didn't touch the head.

208
00:16:08,001 --> 00:16:11,240
In the early 50s, they come out with mandatory hard hats,

209
00:16:11,241 --> 00:16:14,100
brain buckets, whatever you wanted to call them.

210
00:16:14,101 --> 00:16:15,140
You had to wear them then.

211
00:16:15,141 --> 00:16:16,680
And that was a tough thing to sell.

212
00:16:16,681 --> 00:16:17,540
You talk into them.

213
00:16:17,541 --> 00:16:21,880
you get an echo and the old timers didn't like the hard hats, but they realized that

214
00:16:21,881 --> 00:16:26,120
you dropped something on a man's head in a fell hat, it hurts a lot more than it does

215
00:16:26,121 --> 00:16:27,980
when you drop it on somebody with a hard hat.

216
00:16:30,960 --> 00:16:35,460
Throughout the century, linemen searched for more efficient ways to reach the wires.

217
00:16:36,260 --> 00:16:37,860
But most methods were primitive.

218
00:16:39,740 --> 00:16:44,800
Then, in the late 1950s, came the event that is considered the greatest single breakthrough

219
00:16:44,801 --> 00:16:48,764
in line work, the introduction of the bucket truck.

220
00:16:48,765 --> 00:16:51,640
Bucket trucks have made it a lot more

221
00:16:51,641 --> 00:16:52,341
efficient.

222
00:16:52,342 --> 00:16:55,620
You see, you have the mobility to move all around a pole and move out from the pole.

223
00:16:56,660 --> 00:17:00,892
Safer as far as you can get a little bit distance between you and the wire.

224
00:17:00,893 --> 00:17:02,020
You can see a little

225
00:17:02,021 --> 00:17:07,440
more of what's around you. It's increased productivity a lot too because jobs can be

226
00:17:07,441 --> 00:17:08,860
done a lot faster with the buckets.

227
00:17:08,861 --> 00:17:16,080
The insulated bucket truck also led to an astonishing technique

228
00:17:16,081 --> 00:17:20,200
that allowed linemen to grab hot lines with their bare hands

229
00:17:20,201 --> 00:17:29,440
Nearly 950 line workers reportedly died while on the job in the U.S. and Canada

230
00:17:29,441 --> 00:17:31,980
between 1972 and 1986

231
00:17:31,981 --> 00:17:34,580
Modern Marvels will continue in a moment

232
00:17:38,860 --> 00:17:44,780
In 1905, the Niagara Falls power plant transmitted a massive 10,000 volts of electricity.

233
00:17:47,500 --> 00:17:52,460
By 1936, the new Boulder Dam line carried 287,000 volts.

234
00:17:55,020 --> 00:18:01,260
In 1953, the American Electric Power Company built the first 345,000 volt line.

235
00:18:02,860 --> 00:18:08,300
And by the 1970s, 500,000 volt lines crisscrossed the United States.

236
00:18:08,860 --> 00:18:17,140
Working live on this tremendous voltage was difficult, requiring extremely long and cumbersome

237
00:18:17,141 --> 00:18:19,840
hot sticks.

238
00:18:19,841 --> 00:18:25,100
So the utilities developed an astounding new work method called live line bare handing.

239
00:18:25,101 --> 00:18:32,980
With this technique, a super insulated bucket truck carries linemen up to the live wire.

240
00:18:32,981 --> 00:18:39,980
Then, incredibly, they energize their bodies to the same voltage as the line.

241
00:18:39,981 --> 00:18:46,980
A process known as bonding on.

242
00:18:46,981 --> 00:18:51,980
They can then grasp the live wire to do their work.

243
00:18:51,981 --> 00:18:56,980
Because there is no path to the ground, they remain safe.

244
00:18:56,981 --> 00:19:01,700
You've seen birds land on wires all the time.

245
00:19:01,940 --> 00:19:03,640
In theory, that's what we're doing.

246
00:19:03,740 --> 00:19:04,760
We're a bird on the wire.

247
00:19:05,820 --> 00:19:07,820
You will feel no discomfort at all.

248
00:19:09,900 --> 00:19:14,040
But in fact, there are no birds on 500,000 volt wires.

249
00:19:15,120 --> 00:19:20,240
These high voltages create an energy field called induction that surrounds the lines.

250
00:19:21,440 --> 00:19:24,640
This field becomes visible when the lineman bonds on.

251
00:19:24,641 --> 00:19:31,640
Induction is not deadly, but it is uncomfortable for birds and human beings.

252
00:19:31,641 --> 00:19:37,640
To eliminate this discomfort, a special suit was fabricated from steel mesh.

253
00:19:37,641 --> 00:19:43,640
This suit forces the induction to surround the lineman instead of passing through him.

254
00:19:43,641 --> 00:19:50,640
He can then easily handle work like replacing the spacers that keep lines from touching one another.

255
00:19:54,640 --> 00:20:01,960
Bear Handing was first tested in the laboratories of American Electric Power in the 1960s.

256
00:20:02,860 --> 00:20:05,860
An apprehensive linemen were brought in to try it out.

257
00:20:06,980 --> 00:20:08,360
Yes, it's a little nervous.

258
00:20:08,560 --> 00:20:11,720
You go up there and the man's sitting in front of you in the control booth.

259
00:20:12,220 --> 00:20:15,860
And he's telling you, it says, 115,000 volts.

260
00:20:15,980 --> 00:20:17,600
And he begins to sing a little bit.

261
00:20:18,040 --> 00:20:19,020
And he says, 230.

262
00:20:19,760 --> 00:20:21,240
And it's singing more and more.

263
00:20:21,720 --> 00:20:24,440
Next thing you know, he says, 500, bond on.

264
00:20:24,640 --> 00:20:26,540
And you say, what am I doing up here?

265
00:20:31,120 --> 00:20:34,020
The safety record for bear handing has been excellent.

266
00:20:34,840 --> 00:20:37,620
But it is only used in wide open areas,

267
00:20:37,740 --> 00:20:41,520
so there is absolutely no chance of contacting a pass through the ground.

268
00:20:48,880 --> 00:20:54,120
In the late 1950s, helicopters were first used to build transmission lines

269
00:20:54,121 --> 00:20:55,620
in remote wilderness areas.

270
00:21:01,900 --> 00:21:05,540
Then in the 1980s, helicopters and barehanding

271
00:21:05,541 --> 00:21:09,060
combine for a remarkable new way to maintain live lines.

272
00:21:11,020 --> 00:21:13,920
With this technique, the linemen and the helicopter

273
00:21:13,921 --> 00:21:16,240
are both energized to the line voltage.

274
00:21:17,360 --> 00:21:21,320
And again, they are safe because they are not touching a path to the ground.

275
00:21:24,120 --> 00:21:31,520
Helicopter line crews can work much faster than traditional teams on extreme high voltage transmission lines.

276
00:21:33,240 --> 00:21:38,600
Linemen equipped with bucket trucks and hot sticks can replace about eight line spacers in a day.

277
00:21:39,940 --> 00:21:45,780
During the same time, a skilled helicopter team of linemen and pilot can replace over 200.

278
00:21:48,160 --> 00:21:52,760
In my career, I would never have dreamed that I would see a helicopter doing power line work.

279
00:21:54,120 --> 00:21:56,240
I would think it's a little scary the first time you do it, too.

280
00:21:56,300 --> 00:21:58,440
You're sitting out there on a platform on the side of a helicopter,

281
00:21:58,560 --> 00:22:00,160
and you fly right next to a wire,

282
00:22:00,161 --> 00:22:04,180
and you're hoping that the propellers don't get tangled up in anything.

283
00:22:06,040 --> 00:22:08,160
And some of these pilots, I have to commend them.

284
00:22:08,200 --> 00:22:10,480
They can hold that helicopter in the same place

285
00:22:10,481 --> 00:22:12,300
the longest of anything I've ever observed.

286
00:22:14,280 --> 00:22:17,240
These guys are extremely good at what they do, and I commend them.

287
00:22:19,960 --> 00:22:21,700
They do a good job. They're safe.

288
00:22:21,701 --> 00:22:25,680
about the only time they ever have anything happen is when they have an equipment failure.

289
00:22:28,020 --> 00:22:32,500
Line maintenance with helicopters is expensive, and there have been fatalities.

290
00:22:33,180 --> 00:22:37,240
But today, linemen and pilots train for two years to master the technique,

291
00:22:37,740 --> 00:22:40,220
which is considered safe under the right conditions.

292
00:22:41,880 --> 00:22:46,380
However, no safety tools, procedures, or regulations are effective

293
00:22:46,381 --> 00:22:49,260
if a lineman loses his focus and concentration.

294
00:22:51,700 --> 00:23:00,700
In 1987, Stephen Hayes was a young lineman, just six months away from completing his apprenticeship.

295
00:23:00,701 --> 00:23:03,700
This was a long-time goal for Steve.

296
00:23:03,701 --> 00:23:08,700
Like many linemen, he was the third generation of electrical worker in his family.

297
00:23:08,701 --> 00:23:13,700
It was on a beautiful spring day when he let his guard down.

298
00:23:13,701 --> 00:23:18,700
He was in a bucket truck completing work on a 7,600-volt live line,

299
00:23:18,701 --> 00:23:21,920
and he removed his protective rubber gloves.

300
00:23:25,820 --> 00:23:32,020
Apparently there was a failure of a piece of equipment

301
00:23:32,021 --> 00:23:37,740
that came down at him that in reflex he grabbed

302
00:23:37,741 --> 00:23:40,660
and it was an energized conductor.

303
00:23:42,260 --> 00:23:44,780
He had a hand-to-hand contact with electric

304
00:23:44,781 --> 00:23:50,560
and that resulted in internal burning.

305
00:23:50,720 --> 00:23:53,660
When you have a flash, you have external burns.

306
00:23:53,760 --> 00:23:57,240
When you have a contact accident, it damages the bone marrow.

307
00:24:00,440 --> 00:24:02,980
So that's what he had. He had 7,600 volts.

308
00:24:04,760 --> 00:24:07,680
When I made my contact, that was it. I don't remember anything.

309
00:24:09,280 --> 00:24:13,540
Apparently they brought me down, put me on the ground, put the fire out

310
00:24:13,541 --> 00:24:15,280
because I was on fire.

311
00:24:17,020 --> 00:24:20,980
It entered through this arm, came through, wrapped around my neck,

312
00:24:21,220 --> 00:24:24,200
didn't go up, luckily, into my brain or down into my heart,

313
00:24:24,560 --> 00:24:26,060
and went out my other arm.

314
00:24:26,300 --> 00:24:30,580
So, I mean, if you're lucky, in that kind of a situation, I was pretty lucky.

315
00:24:32,260 --> 00:24:35,360
The hospital was rough because the problem with the burn injury,

316
00:24:35,361 --> 00:24:38,960
I believe electrical and any other burn, is infection right away.

317
00:24:39,840 --> 00:24:43,780
And so they wanted me to make a decision of amputation right away,

318
00:24:43,940 --> 00:24:45,200
and it was very tough.

319
00:24:45,280 --> 00:24:46,880
I couldn't make that decision up.

320
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:48,380
I mean, I knew it had to be done.

321
00:24:48,440 --> 00:24:51,140
I just couldn't bring myself to say, yeah, cut my arms off.

322
00:24:53,100 --> 00:24:56,880
It took about three days until I came around and allowed him to operate.

323
00:24:59,480 --> 00:25:02,420
Steve would spend eight weeks in the hospital burn unit.

324
00:25:03,120 --> 00:25:06,440
He then endured three years of painful rehabilitation,

325
00:25:06,441 --> 00:25:09,380
learning to use his prosthetic arms.

326
00:25:10,700 --> 00:25:13,820
Then in 1991, he returned to work,

327
00:25:14,340 --> 00:25:17,180
now as an electrical line inspector.

328
00:25:18,460 --> 00:25:19,720
I was a little scared.

329
00:25:19,940 --> 00:25:23,500
I didn't know how I'd feel being back around the job

330
00:25:23,501 --> 00:25:26,320
and the equipment that I got hurt on.

331
00:25:27,740 --> 00:25:29,740
And I was a little embarrassed to come back.

332
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:33,400
For one, I didn't follow the safety rules.

333
00:25:33,401 --> 00:25:35,660
If I had followed the safety rules at that time,

334
00:25:35,661 --> 00:25:38,300
we wouldn't be having this conversation, obviously.

335
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:43,780
That's all I had to do was wear my gloves.

336
00:25:43,900 --> 00:25:46,020
And you're not talking about a big piece of equipment either

337
00:25:46,021 --> 00:25:50,780
that you have to, you know, struggle to put on and struggle to take off.

338
00:25:50,860 --> 00:25:52,100
You're talking about a pair of gloves.

339
00:25:56,520 --> 00:25:59,060
For the amount of time it took me to take my gloves off,

340
00:25:59,720 --> 00:26:01,420
it took less time to lose my arms.

341
00:26:01,421 --> 00:26:11,100
Steve also gave safety lectures to the utility industry, and his story delivered a powerful message to fellow linemen.

342
00:26:11,101 --> 00:26:19,420
obviously they realize what they're dealing with and kind of brings them closer together i think

343
00:26:19,421 --> 00:26:24,660
as linemen i think there's a certain amount of pride in the fact that they're linemen

344
00:26:24,661 --> 00:26:31,660
they understand their jobs hazardous and when somebody gets hurt it makes them all step back

345
00:26:31,661 --> 00:26:41,080
and think i give steve a lot of credit uh to come through an accident like that with the amount of

346
00:26:41,081 --> 00:26:44,860
devastation that it caused him and his family

347
00:26:44,861 --> 00:26:46,760
and then to be able to come back and still work.

348
00:26:47,700 --> 00:26:49,000
I give him a lot of credit for that.

349
00:26:50,460 --> 00:26:52,760
I guess having somebody like Steve around,

350
00:26:54,340 --> 00:26:56,640
anybody that doesn't think that electric can hurt you,

351
00:26:57,640 --> 00:26:59,240
all you have to do is look.

352
00:27:02,760 --> 00:27:05,920
Stephen Hayes is now happily married with two young children,

353
00:27:06,760 --> 00:27:10,280
and he wouldn't stand in the way of his kids becoming linemen.

354
00:27:11,080 --> 00:27:14,966
It's a great job. It's very rewarding.

355
00:27:14,967 --> 00:27:20,080
You're at different locations, you're outside. So,

356
00:27:20,081 --> 00:27:24,880
as a career, I would recommend it. As long as you pay attention, as long as you take

357
00:27:24,881 --> 00:27:29,080
care of yourself, it's a great job.

358
00:27:29,081 --> 00:27:35,380
Line workers can physically grasp and work on wires charged with up to 750,000 volts of

359
00:27:35,381 --> 00:27:37,586
electricity without sustaining injury.

360
00:27:37,587 --> 00:27:40,780
We'll be back with more High Voltage on Modern Marvels.

361
00:27:41,080 --> 00:27:49,820
On rare occasions, major power lines are shut down for maintenance.

362
00:27:50,760 --> 00:27:58,840
This 500,000 volt transmission line that runs 580 miles through California is turned off for one week a year.

363
00:28:00,200 --> 00:28:05,280
During that time, every available lineman is dispatched to work as quickly as possible.

364
00:28:05,281 --> 00:28:11,280
This crew from the Los Angeles Department of Water and Power is fixing a section that

365
00:28:11,281 --> 00:28:13,280
traverses the Mojave Desert.

366
00:28:13,281 --> 00:28:19,080
What we were doing was using this, what we call a conductor cart, and we go from tower

367
00:28:19,081 --> 00:28:23,960
to tower on the wire itself repairing the conductor spacers.

368
00:28:23,961 --> 00:28:29,680
And the preformed spacers, they do break due to the wind and vibration, and occasionally

369
00:28:29,681 --> 00:28:31,360
we have to change them out.

370
00:28:31,361 --> 00:28:38,360
The danger now is not the current, but the tremendous height and howling wind.

371
00:28:38,361 --> 00:28:46,360
You think it's windy down here, we're all getting blown around, but up there it's multiplied.

372
00:28:46,361 --> 00:28:48,360
Very dangerous.

373
00:28:48,361 --> 00:28:56,360
Meanwhile, 50 miles away, another crew replaces a damaged insulator.

374
00:28:56,361 --> 00:29:04,640
Whether the line is live or dead, linemen rely on precise teamwork and strict attention

375
00:29:04,641 --> 00:29:10,120
to detail to complete their job efficiently and safely.

376
00:29:10,121 --> 00:29:22,300
One little minor thing can cause a major accident and that's usually how it happens.

377
00:29:22,301 --> 00:29:23,700
is overlooked during the process.

378
00:29:24,820 --> 00:29:26,800
And then you lose control of the situation.

379
00:29:26,801 --> 00:29:30,340
And if you're lucky, it doesn't get you too bad.

380
00:29:30,660 --> 00:29:32,720
You learn a real cheap lesson.

381
00:29:33,380 --> 00:29:35,620
And in a lot of cases, you know, people get hurt.

382
00:29:36,660 --> 00:29:37,360
Coming up!

383
00:29:37,860 --> 00:29:40,640
Working a line de-energized is highly unusual.

384
00:29:41,620 --> 00:29:44,500
Today, customers demand uninterrupted service.

385
00:29:47,320 --> 00:29:49,800
This roadway is about to be widened.

386
00:29:49,801 --> 00:29:56,800
A crew from Georgia Power must carefully move live 20,000-volt lines to new poles.

387
00:29:56,801 --> 00:30:05,800
The reason we're doing it energized, if we didn't, we'd have to kill a whole area of customers, so we'd have a major outage.

388
00:30:05,801 --> 00:30:10,800
So in order to keep all of our customers with service, we're doing it energized.

389
00:30:10,801 --> 00:30:17,800
The first step is a safety briefing, known as a tailgate meeting.

390
00:30:36,220 --> 00:30:38,800
The crew then dons their safety gear.

391
00:30:38,801 --> 00:30:45,240
Anytime you're in an energized area, you need to have also have on a fire retardant clothing,

392
00:30:45,380 --> 00:30:46,580
either a shirt or a jacket.

393
00:30:47,580 --> 00:30:52,280
When you go up into an airlift device, a bucket truck, you have to have on your fall protection.

394
00:30:57,900 --> 00:31:00,880
The linemen test their all-important rubber gloves.

395
00:31:02,160 --> 00:31:06,000
Kind of blow it up and listen for air leaks.

396
00:31:06,001 --> 00:31:11,120
and also you notice the inside is a different color than the outside if it has a deep cut in it

397
00:31:11,121 --> 00:31:18,460
then it'll uh the yellow will show through the black and you expand the rubber look for look for

398
00:31:18,461 --> 00:31:26,260
the yellow and listen for leaks if you come up with a short piece of line hole two foot all paths

399
00:31:26,261 --> 00:31:31,320
to the ground must be carefully covered when we get into an energizer there one of the first things

400
00:31:31,321 --> 00:31:39,720
want to do is cover up the bear conductors, make sure we allow for incidental contact,

401
00:31:39,721 --> 00:31:45,080
unanticipated moves so to speak. So we slide these onto the bear conductor.

402
00:31:45,081 --> 00:31:54,000
Cover up the pole, different hardware. We call it a rubber blanket. It's made out of

403
00:31:54,001 --> 00:31:56,536
the same material as the line hose.

404
00:31:56,537 --> 00:32:00,160
You just wrap this up, wrap it around the whatever

405
00:32:00,161 --> 00:32:02,880
you're covering up the pole or the arm or whatever.

406
00:32:03,460 --> 00:32:07,400
You can have some giant clothespins to hold them into place.

407
00:32:11,140 --> 00:32:15,080
An insulated arm assists in inching the lines to the new poles.

408
00:32:18,000 --> 00:32:21,880
The hot wires must not touch the wood or any other conductor.

409
00:32:22,620 --> 00:32:26,600
At these high voltages, a flash can be as deadly as making contact.

410
00:32:26,601 --> 00:32:32,000
That being all ground potential, that'd be like putting two battery cables together.

411
00:32:32,900 --> 00:32:34,780
Just in a higher scale.

412
00:32:35,660 --> 00:32:38,060
We'll have a heck of a ball of fire up here if that happens.

413
00:32:38,500 --> 00:32:39,300
I mean a monster.

414
00:32:41,580 --> 00:32:45,040
The linemen safely finished the job in just three hours,

415
00:32:45,500 --> 00:32:49,420
a process that would have taken two days working with hot sticks off the poles.

416
00:32:52,300 --> 00:32:54,600
This crew was able to work in good weather,

417
00:32:55,380 --> 00:33:00,260
But it is the goal of every lineman to keep the power up, whatever the conditions.

418
00:33:01,260 --> 00:33:08,900
I think most linemen have a firm belief instilled in them that reliability of the electrical system is premier,

419
00:33:09,060 --> 00:33:14,680
and that's their cause in life, in their working lives, is to maintain reliability of the electrical system.

420
00:33:14,681 --> 00:33:23,360
Early on, pioneering electrical workers learned that all their hard work can be destroyed

421
00:33:23,361 --> 00:33:28,600
in an instant by bad weather or natural disasters.

422
00:33:28,601 --> 00:33:33,920
The lineman's job then is to head into the teeth of the problem and restore power.

423
00:33:33,921 --> 00:33:40,080
In the storm, you just went right out in there and worked the same as you did any other time.

424
00:33:40,081 --> 00:33:44,320
Worked it with sticks or with your gloves and any, all kind of weather.

425
00:33:44,420 --> 00:33:45,120
It didn't matter.

426
00:33:45,800 --> 00:33:46,580
Ice storms.

427
00:33:47,040 --> 00:33:49,320
I climbed ice six inches thick on poles.

428
00:33:51,520 --> 00:33:56,320
Working disasters today presents the same challenges it did decades ago.

429
00:33:57,620 --> 00:34:03,340
The 1991 Oakland Hills fire burned down 3,000 homes and killed 20 people.

430
00:34:03,341 --> 00:34:14,340
Thousands were left without power as an awesome 220 tons of poles, wires and transformers were destroyed by the blaze.

431
00:34:14,341 --> 00:34:19,340
Restoring power was a daunting task.

432
00:34:19,341 --> 00:34:26,340
Crews from Pacific Gas and Electric replaced over 1,000 poles in a matter of days.

433
00:34:26,341 --> 00:34:33,340
Even transmission tower insulators had to be washed because the ash was conductive and

434
00:34:33,341 --> 00:34:38,340
could have caused flash-overs shorting out the lines.

435
00:34:38,341 --> 00:34:44,340
Four years later, the same PG&E crews were handling torrential rain and flood damage

436
00:34:44,341 --> 00:34:46,191
north of San Francisco.

437
00:34:46,192 --> 00:34:51,340
Trees and limbs were tearing down lines and poles were toppling.

438
00:34:51,341 --> 00:34:56,100
These heroic crews braved 100-mile-an-hour winds on poles,

439
00:34:56,101 --> 00:34:58,540
and many worked 40 hours straight.

440
00:35:01,800 --> 00:35:04,980
After the 1994 Northridge earthquake in Los Angeles,

441
00:35:05,660 --> 00:35:06,360
the L.A.

442
00:35:06,361 --> 00:35:09,760
Department of Water and Power rushed to the site of a downed transmission tower.

443
00:35:12,000 --> 00:35:17,680
When I got up there, just assessing the damage and looking at what happened,

444
00:35:17,681 --> 00:35:20,380
And I said, this place rocketed and rolled, man.

445
00:35:20,440 --> 00:35:22,340
This must have really had a good hit.

446
00:35:24,360 --> 00:35:28,600
The linemen prepared to climb the mangled structure and move the critical lines.

447
00:35:29,540 --> 00:35:31,940
Then, a massive aftershock hit.

448
00:35:32,980 --> 00:35:37,460
But when the aftershock hit, it was so severe there that you think,

449
00:35:37,540 --> 00:35:40,780
wow, I don't want to be moving wire with these aftershocks.

450
00:35:40,880 --> 00:35:42,380
And, you know, what's the ground going to do?

451
00:35:42,520 --> 00:35:45,320
It was almost like this ground was just going to collapse down.

452
00:35:47,680 --> 00:35:51,820
But the linemen braved the unstable earth and scaled the tower.

453
00:35:52,520 --> 00:35:59,000
They placed one group of lines in a crane and moved the others to an emergency structure known as a Lindsay Tower.

454
00:36:00,680 --> 00:36:09,860
I remember I was up halfway up the Lindsay structure and a huge aftershock took place there and the whole thing shook.

455
00:36:10,560 --> 00:36:16,880
After a while, you felt that what we're working with was pretty solid on it and we kind of gained confidence.

456
00:36:17,680 --> 00:36:22,840
After just eight hours, power was restored to the vital lines.

457
00:36:25,520 --> 00:36:27,600
You got to hand it to the guys that did it.

458
00:36:27,700 --> 00:36:29,640
I mean, they were up there and they were fighting,

459
00:36:29,780 --> 00:36:31,640
and these quakes were coming afterward,

460
00:36:32,000 --> 00:36:34,620
and it was pretty exciting when you're on top of that hill

461
00:36:34,621 --> 00:36:39,900
and that crane is sitting there with this hot 220,000 up there above your head,

462
00:36:39,901 --> 00:36:41,760
and it's rattling.

463
00:36:41,920 --> 00:36:43,780
You don't know it's going to be another big one or not.

464
00:36:43,781 --> 00:36:50,780
This devastating quake marked the only time in history all of Los Angeles lost power.

465
00:36:50,781 --> 00:36:57,780
But in just 24 hours, DWP crews had restored 90% of the system.

466
00:36:57,781 --> 00:37:01,780
In recent years, utilities have established a system of mutual aid,

467
00:37:01,781 --> 00:37:06,780
where Lyman traveled to other parts of the country to assist in emergency restoration.

468
00:37:06,781 --> 00:37:13,880
In 1998, a massive ice storm paralyzed the northeast United States and parts of Canada.

469
00:37:17,720 --> 00:37:20,680
Line crews arrived from as far away as Hawaii.

470
00:37:23,280 --> 00:37:25,380
We were there for about 29 days.

471
00:37:26,340 --> 00:37:28,580
And the devastation up there was horrendous.

472
00:37:29,220 --> 00:37:30,300
Poles were down for miles.

473
00:37:30,920 --> 00:37:34,340
We basically had to rebuild their whole utility system.

474
00:37:34,341 --> 00:37:41,340
Disaster work is a lineman's greatest challenge, but it also offers the greatest rewards.

475
00:37:41,341 --> 00:37:43,014
There is a rush to it.

476
00:37:43,015 --> 00:37:48,340
I think everybody gets a lot more out of it when you know that you're,

477
00:37:48,341 --> 00:37:49,868
you know, people are out of power.

478
00:37:49,869 --> 00:37:52,340
Sometimes it's a small area, sometimes it's a big area.

479
00:37:52,341 --> 00:37:53,503
It's a major storm.

480
00:37:53,504 --> 00:37:58,340
And to get as many people back as soon as you can, yeah, there is a rush to it.

481
00:37:58,341 --> 00:38:01,340
It makes you feel good.

482
00:38:04,340 --> 00:38:09,720
Over the years, the utilities and the electrical union have taken dramatic steps to ensure

483
00:38:09,721 --> 00:38:12,940
the well-being of every lineman.

484
00:38:12,941 --> 00:38:18,320
In fact, research at Johns Hopkins and other universities that led to modern CPR techniques

485
00:38:18,321 --> 00:38:25,060
and equipment was originally sponsored by the electrical utility industry to save linemen.

486
00:38:25,061 --> 00:38:32,700
Today, workers must undergo years of formal training and apprenticeship before they can

487
00:38:32,701 --> 00:38:38,520
claim the title of journeyman, a professional lineman.

488
00:38:38,521 --> 00:38:42,240
Most of them agree that training is the key to safety,

489
00:38:42,241 --> 00:38:44,700
and they never stop trying to improve their methods

490
00:38:44,701 --> 00:38:45,940
of protecting themselves.

491
00:38:50,460 --> 00:38:52,420
Linemen from the Georgia Power Company

492
00:38:52,421 --> 00:38:58,300
cautiously approach 500,000 volt transmission lines.

493
00:38:58,301 --> 00:39:01,000
Then, using the bare hand technique,

494
00:39:01,001 --> 00:39:08,000
They energize their own bodies, grasp the live wires, and climb out onto the slender perch.

495
00:39:10,840 --> 00:39:14,520
These linemen are not up here to fix these power lines.

496
00:39:14,521 --> 00:39:17,700
This is a special training exercise.

497
00:39:17,701 --> 00:39:23,680
They are attempting to rescue a co-worker trapped on the cinder bundle of wires.

498
00:39:23,681 --> 00:39:28,680
This risky procedure has never been tried, but the training is crucial.

499
00:39:31,000 --> 00:39:33,660
It can take hours to shut down a major line like this,

500
00:39:34,220 --> 00:39:37,000
so the linemen know that if they are injured up here,

501
00:39:37,540 --> 00:39:40,080
only a fellow lineman can save their lives.

502
00:39:43,060 --> 00:39:45,440
Fire departments, emergency services,

503
00:39:45,800 --> 00:39:49,240
they're not going to be able to cope with a transmission line injury

504
00:39:49,241 --> 00:39:52,260
or accident or medical call,

505
00:39:52,600 --> 00:39:55,380
simply because that's beyond the scope of their training.

506
00:39:55,500 --> 00:39:56,780
They are not going to go up there.

507
00:39:56,860 --> 00:40:00,400
They're going to say, you get the man down to us

508
00:40:00,401 --> 00:40:01,680
and we will take him from there.

509
00:40:03,380 --> 00:40:06,780
Assisting the linemen in this training exercise is Reed Thorne.

510
00:40:07,540 --> 00:40:10,320
Reed is a world-renowned instructor in rope rescue,

511
00:40:11,140 --> 00:40:15,680
techniques he uses to save lives in mountaineering and industrial accidents.

512
00:40:17,380 --> 00:40:18,600
Come on now, pull harder.

513
00:40:20,840 --> 00:40:23,280
This rescue is especially treacherous

514
00:40:23,281 --> 00:40:26,640
because they cannot just lower the victim straight down with a rope.

515
00:40:26,641 --> 00:40:33,840
At these enormous voltages, the lineman on the wire doesn't even have to touch the tower

516
00:40:33,841 --> 00:40:36,640
to complete a path to the ground.

517
00:40:36,641 --> 00:40:41,500
If he gets within 11 feet 3 inches of the steel, the electricity will surge through

518
00:40:41,501 --> 00:40:45,440
his body and jump to the tower.

519
00:40:45,441 --> 00:40:51,640
The wire sits just 14 feet from the structure, so the margin for error is slim.

520
00:40:51,641 --> 00:40:56,580
If you were to violate the 11-foot-3 clearance by reaching in toward the tower,

521
00:40:57,160 --> 00:41:00,540
there should be an electrical arc between you and the tower.

522
00:41:02,560 --> 00:41:04,240
Theoretically, you'd be a puff of smoke.

523
00:41:06,740 --> 00:41:10,900
The other linemen in the crew climb the tower to set the intricate rigging.

524
00:41:12,280 --> 00:41:15,900
The plan calls for one rope to lower the victim off the wire,

525
00:41:16,340 --> 00:41:20,500
while a tracking line down to a truck pulls him clear of the danger zone.

526
00:41:21,640 --> 00:41:35,463
If the tracking element fails, they immediately swing back into the 11 foot 3 clearance that we were supposed to maintain and they would instantly be, well, I think

527
00:41:35,464 --> 00:41:38,160
you know it would be disastrous.

528
00:41:39,540 --> 00:41:44,480
The men delicately lower their comrade, knowing they cannot let him slip.

529
00:41:46,480 --> 00:41:51,160
How's it look from over there? Is it far enough? You've got a good angle over there.

530
00:41:51,640 --> 00:41:53,960
I'm fine. I can tell right here he's fine.

531
00:41:54,640 --> 00:41:58,300
This may appear to be a high-stakes exercise for these linemen,

532
00:41:58,680 --> 00:42:03,060
but to them, it was worth the risk to add another measure of safety

533
00:42:03,061 --> 00:42:05,320
to their extremely hazardous job.

534
00:42:06,220 --> 00:42:07,400
We got you now, buddy.

535
00:42:07,960 --> 00:42:08,660
Woo-ha!

536
00:42:09,320 --> 00:42:10,760
Oh, track and line.

537
00:42:12,040 --> 00:42:12,880
Oh, that was great.

538
00:42:13,000 --> 00:42:16,920
That was probably the most satisfying experience I ever had in line work,

539
00:42:16,921 --> 00:42:18,780
knowing that you could actually go up there

540
00:42:18,781 --> 00:42:22,500
and pick somebody off the bundle, walk him out the line and down

541
00:42:22,501 --> 00:42:24,400
with no more strain than what we had.

542
00:42:27,300 --> 00:42:31,320
Certainly, line work has come a long way from its primitive origins.

543
00:42:31,880 --> 00:42:34,960
But the old skills haven't completely disappeared.

544
00:42:36,380 --> 00:42:39,040
This is the International Linemen's Rodeo,

545
00:42:39,041 --> 00:42:44,260
an annual event where linemen from around the world gather to honor their profession.

546
00:42:44,261 --> 00:42:51,260
They demonstrate their unique abilities by competing in events of speed and skill.

547
00:42:51,261 --> 00:42:55,260
They also recognize the ever-present hazards.

548
00:42:55,261 --> 00:43:00,260
One event is a race to rescue an injured co-worker from a pole.

549
00:43:00,261 --> 00:43:05,260
And like the linemen that first gathered more than a century ago,

550
00:43:05,261 --> 00:43:08,260
they trade stories and techniques.

551
00:43:08,261 --> 00:43:15,340
And most of all, they celebrate the knowledge that they are members of a special brotherhood.

552
00:43:16,660 --> 00:43:19,200
If you're a lineman, I think you've got a lot to be proud of.

553
00:43:19,920 --> 00:43:28,420
Not everybody can go out and put rubber gloves on every day and touch live conductors and move them safely and go home and see their folks.

554
00:43:29,820 --> 00:43:31,900
Any lineman should be proud of what he does.

555
00:43:38,260 --> 00:43:43,260
You go up an old, gnarly old pole and make things safe

556
00:43:43,800 --> 00:43:46,660
and fix it up with new cross arms and transformers

557
00:43:46,661 --> 00:43:49,380
and switches and insulators and guy wires.

558
00:43:50,380 --> 00:43:52,100
When you go by there, if it was a tough job,

559
00:43:52,101 --> 00:43:54,340
you go, hey man, I did that, you know, it's kinda cool.

560
00:43:55,660 --> 00:43:59,320
The downside is injuries and friends gone.

561
00:44:03,100 --> 00:44:05,820
You miss it, you miss what you did,

562
00:44:05,821 --> 00:44:10,120
You miss the companionship with all the people you work with.

563
00:44:11,120 --> 00:44:14,540
You look at it now, it wasn't so pleasurable when it was happening.

564
00:44:15,400 --> 00:44:19,020
But you think back on it, and you did it.

565
00:44:19,220 --> 00:44:20,320
And that's the main thing.

566
00:44:20,620 --> 00:44:21,360
You survived.

